    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d177-a1">

                <pb facs="0269" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1866p2/pg195"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 1.</seg></opener>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-missing-head-in-printed-volume">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States in
                                Japan,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Yedo,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-12-16"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 16, 1865</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have the honor to inform you that I this
                    day received a letter from the Gorogio, in which it is stated that, “although
                    greatly rejoiced at the Mikado’s sanction to the treaties having been obtained,
                    by which the relations between the United States and Japan have been placed on
                    an improved basis, yet their anxiety has also greatly increased in view of
                    proceedings having for their object a rupture of friendly relations with their
                    government, being probably contemplated by persons who feel keenly disappointed
                    at this solemn act of the Mikado.”</p>
                <p>It is clearly the duty of the Japanese government to control such lawless
                    persons. The Gorogio have been distinctly informed to that effect; and also,
                    that for any failure in this respect the United States will hold their
                    government to a strict responsibility.</p>
                <p>I request you to make the foregoing known to all citizens of the United States
                    within your jurisdiction, and to recommend the observance of constant
                    watchfulness against such outbreaks.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>A. L. C. PORTMAN.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">George S. Fisher,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">United States Consul, Kanagawa.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
